@page "/privacy"
@* The privacy policy for the live site. Google's sign-in and the Help page link here, so it has to
   describe the app and the hosting as they are. Change the date at the top when the content changes. *@

<PageTitle>Privacy · Stikling</PageTitle>

<a class="back-link" href="settings">Settings</a>
<h1>Privacy</h1>

<p class="privacy-updated">Last changed 30 September 2026</p>

<p>
    Stikling is a free app that I make and run as a hobby project. I'm
    <a href="https://github.com/LaugeM">LaugeM on GitHub</a>, and you can reach me at
    <a href="mailto:support@stikling.app">support@stikling.app</a>. This page says what happens to what you
    put into the app.
</p>

<section class="privacy-section">
    <h2>Without an account</h2>
    <p>
        Everything you add stays on your device, in the browser's own storage. None of it is sent anywhere.
        The app is served by GitHub Pages, which sees each visit the way any website host does, including
        your IP address. <a href="https://docs.github.com/site-policy/privacy-policies/github-general-privacy-statement">GitHub's privacy statement</a>
        covers that.
    </p>
</section>

<section class="privacy-section">
    <h2>With an account</h2>
    <p>
        An account is optional, and only needed to keep the same plants on several devices.
    </p>
    <p>
        <strong>Signing in</strong> is handled by <a href="https://clerk.com/legal/privacy">Clerk</a>, a company
        in the United States. Clerk keeps your email address, and your name and profile picture if you sign in
        with Google, along with when you signed in. If you sign in with your email, Clerk sends the code to it.
    </p>
    <p>
        <strong>What you add in the app</strong>, like plants, propagations, care, pests, photos and settings, is
        sent to the Stikling server so your other devices get it too. The server runs on Microsoft Azure in
        Sweden. Only people in your collection can get at it through the app, and for now that is only you.
        As the one who runs the server I can technically reach the database, but I only look at it to fix a
        problem you've told me about.
    </p>
</section>

<section class="privacy-section">
    <h2>What Stikling doesn't do</h2>
    <p>
        There are no ads, no analytics and no tracking. Your data isn't sold or shared with anyone other than
        the services named on this page, and it's only used to run the app. The app doesn't use cookies of its
        own. Clerk sets the cookies it needs to keep you signed in.
    </p>
</section>

<section class="privacy-section">
    <h2>How long it's kept</h2>
    <p>
        As long as you have the account. When you delete it under Account in Settings, your plants, photos
        and settings are erased from the server straight away, and Clerk deletes your sign-in. The database's
        automatic backups still hold a copy for up to 7 days, after which it's gone. The server keeps a note
        that the account was deleted, without your email or anything else about you, so a device that is still
        signed in can't upload it all again.
    </p>
</section>

<section class="privacy-section">
    <h2>Your rights</h2>
    <p>
        You can get a copy of everything as a backup under Backup in Settings, change anything in the app,
        and delete your account whenever you like. For anything else, like a question about what is kept,
        write to <a href="mailto:support@stikling.app">support@stikling.app</a>. If you think your data is
        handled wrongly, you can complain to the Danish Data Protection Agency,
        <a href="https://www.datatilsynet.dk">Datatilsynet</a>.
    </p>
</section>
